Dataset cf14071_repro {
  label: 'CF-14071 repro (customer diagram)'
  description: '''
    The customer's diagram, box for box and line for line. 8 models, 8 relationships, nothing else.

      Dim Store            -> Fact Sales, Fact SOH
      Dim Region           -> Fact Sales, Fact SOH
      Dim Date             -> Fact Sales, Fact Sales (Benchmark)
      Dim Region (Benchmark) -> Fact Sales (Benchmark), Fact SOH (Benchmark)

    The RLS rule is on Dim Store. There is deliberately NO relationship from Dim Store to either benchmark
    fact - the diagram's "No Path" arrow.

    EVERY RELATIONSHIP IS 'one_way'. This is what reproduces the customer's error, and it is the standard
    galaxy-schema setting: users filter/group facts by dimensions (dim -> fact) but cannot combine benchmark
    metrics with core store dimensions. rlp_propagation is left unset, so it defaults to 'inherit' and
    follows filter_direction - meaning permission filters also flow one_way, from the 'one' side (dimension)
    to the 'many' side (fact), and never back up.

    Consequence, which is exactly the customer's complaint. RLP leaves Dim Store, reaches Fact Sales and
    Fact SOH, and STOPS. It cannot climb from Fact Sales up into Dim Date, so it never crosses to the
    benchmark side. These three models are unreachable from the rule - the diagram's three pink boxes:

      - Fact Sales (Benchmark)
      - Fact SOH (Benchmark)
      - Dim Region (Benchmark)

    Any Explore touching them fails with:
      "Some permission rules are not applied in this Explore. This is likely due to a missing relationship
       between the models."

    That is the blocker: the customer wants these three explicitly marked out-of-scope for the rule, and
    there is no AML construct for that today.

    WHY NOT THE two_way DEFAULT. If you drop 'one_way' and let everything default to two_way, there is NO
    error at all - RLP walks Dim Store -> Fact Sales -> UP into Dim Date -> DOWN into Fact Sales (Benchmark)
    -> UP into Dim Region (Benchmark) -> DOWN into Fact SOH (Benchmark), so every model is reachable. But the
    benchmark cohort then gets silently narrowed by the store entitlement, which is a wrong number instead of
    an error. Both failure modes are unacceptable to the customer; this dataset shows the first one.

    Fixtures. Stores 1-5 (Store A-E); Fact SOH only carries stores 1-3.
    With vendor_id = 3 (Store C), Sales $ = 500 (100 + 400). The benchmark measures cannot be queried at all
    here - that is the point.

    RLP is not enforced in dev and Admins bypass it - publish to production and use "View and Edit as" a
    non-admin with vendor_id set, or the error will not appear.
  '''
  data_source_name: 'demodb'

  models: [
    dim_store,
    dim_region,
    dim_date,
    dim_region_benchmark,
    fact_sales,
    fact_soh,
    fact_sales_benchmark,
    fact_soh_benchmark,
  ]

  relationships: [
    // Dim Store -> core facts. The RLS rule enters here and can go no further:
    // one_way means RLP flows dim -> fact only, so it cannot climb back out of these facts.
    relationship(fact_sales.store_key > dim_store.store_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_soh.store_key > dim_store.store_key, true, 'one_way'),

    // Dim Region -> core facts.
    relationship(fact_sales.region_key > dim_region.region_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_soh.region_key > dim_region.region_key, true, 'one_way'),

    // Dim Date -> Fact Sales and Fact Sales (Benchmark). Neither SOH fact connects.
    // This is the edge that would bridge core -> benchmark if it were two_way. It is not.
    relationship(fact_sales.date_key > dim_date.date_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_sales_benchmark.date_key > dim_date.date_key, true, 'one_way'),

    // Dim Region (Benchmark) -> benchmark facts. Nothing here leads back to Dim Store.
    relationship(fact_sales_benchmark.region_key > dim_region_benchmark.region_key, true, 'one_way'),
    relationship(fact_soh_benchmark.region_key > dim_region_benchmark.region_key, true, 'one_way'),
  ]

  // vendor_id stands in for the customer's ga_sr_storeid, which does not exist in this workspace.
  permission store_reporting_proprietor_permissions {
    field: r(dim_store.store_key)
    operator: 'matches_user_attribute'
    value: 'vendor_id'
  }
}
